        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best air bike for home?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most homes, the Schwinn AirDyne AD7: a belt drive that reviewers found quiet for an air bike, a 350 lb rating, a 10-year frame warranty and a battery console, for $1,099 on sale. Sunny's fan bike is a belt-driven alternative for $699.99, XMark's CAT 5 Hurricane the commercial-style one on Amazon, and Marcy's AIR-1 the cheapest from an established brand.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best assault bike?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              "Assault bike" usually means any fan bike with moving arms. Of Assault Fitness's own bikes, the Pro X is the best buy for most homes because it has a quieter, lower-maintenance belt drive and Bluetooth for $899. The Classic is $150 cheaper on sale but chain-driven; the Elite costs $1,499. Assault sells them direct rather than on Amazon; the closest belt-driven fan bike we found on Amazon is Sunny's, at $699.99.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a belt-drive or chain-drive air bike better?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For indoor use, belt drive. Belts are quieter and need no lubrication. Chains are cheaper, strong and easy to service, but rattle and need lubricating and tension checks roughly monthly. Neither changes the fan noise, which is the loudest part of any air bike.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are air bikes loud?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. The fan has to move air to create resistance, so every air bike is loud during hard efforts, with a sound reviewers compare to a vacuum cleaner. A belt drive removes chain rattle and a mat stops vibration reaching the floor, but neither silences the fan. For shared walls, a magnetic exercise bike is quieter.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Concept2 BikeErg an air bike?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It uses air resistance from a flywheel, but it has fixed handlebars, so it is a legs-only bike rather than a fan bike with moving arms. It is lighter and smaller than any fan bike here, rides like a road bike and has a damper for changing the feel.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much space does an air bike need?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Plan for roughly 4 to 4.5 feet by 2 feet of floor for the frame, plus room for the handles to swing and your elbows. The Titan is about 20 inches wide and Sunny gives its fan bike as 55.5 by 25.2 inches; a retailer gives the XMark CAT 5 as 53 by 26 inches. Check the maker's ceiling figure in a low basement.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
